NNAAMMEE
     ffoolldd — fold long lines for finite width output device

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffoolldd [--bbss] [--ww _w_i_d_t_h] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffoolldd utility is a filter which folds the contents of the specified
     files, or the standard input if no files are specified, breaking the
     lines to have a maximum of 80 columns.

     The options are as follows:

     --bb      Count _w_i_d_t_h in bytes rather than column positions.

     --ss      Fold line after the last blank character within the first _w_i_d_t_h
             column positions (or bytes).

     --ww _w_i_d_t_h
             Specify a line width to use instead of the default 80 columns.
             The _w_i_d_t_h value should be a multiple of 8 if tabs are present, or
             the tabs should be expanded using expand(1) before using ffoolldd.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The LANG, LC_ALL and LC_CTYPE environment variables affect the execution
     of ffoolldd as described in environ(7).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Fold text in standard input with a width of 20 columns:

           $ echo "I am smart enough to know that I am dumb" | fold -w 15
           I am smart enou
           gh to know that
            I am dumb

     Same as above but breaking lines after the last blank character:

           $ echo "I am smart enough to know that I am dumb" | fold -s -w 15
           I am smart
           enough to know
           that I am dumb

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     expand(1), fmt(1)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ffoolldd utility conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2001 (“POSIX.1”).

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ffoolldd utility first appeared in 1BSD.  It was rewritten for
     4.3BSD-Reno to improve speed and modernize style.  The --bb and --ss options
     were added to NetBSD 1.0 for IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”) compliance.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Bill Joy wrote the original version of ffoolldd on June 28, 1977.  Kevin
     Ruddy rewrote the command in 1990, and J. T. Conklin added the missing
     options in 1993.

BBUUGGSS
     If underlining (see ul(1)) is present it may be messed up by folding.

